### FabLog.TrustHub — every endpoint, for pre-flight and poking by hand.
### VS Code (REST Client extension) and Visual Studio 2026 both render a
### "Send request" link above each block below. The same calls as the curl
### commands in SETUP.md, with less to retype.
###
### ⚠️ https, matching TrustHub's Program.cs and launchSettings.json.
### Requires the ASP.NET Core dev cert to be trusted once per machine:
### `dotnet dev-certs https --trust` — see SETUP.md §0.

@baseUrl = https://localhost:5100

### Pre-flight — expect notes:12, seeded:12, cloudConfigured:true
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### Everything the hub is holding — diagnostics only
GET {{baseUrl}}/notes

### Every submitted shift report
GET {{baseUrl}}/reports

### Back to the seed corpus alone — 12 notes, 0 reports
POST {{baseUrl}}/reset

### Push a note directly, bypassing FabPad — handy for seeding a scenario
### without going through the UI. Mirrors what HubClient.SyncAsync posts.
POST {{baseUrl}}/sync
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
  "author": "K. Nagy",
  "shift": "Night 2026-09-14",
  "toolId": "ETCH-03",
  "chamber": "B",
  "metric": "uniformity",
  "severity": "Escalate",
  "text": "ETCH-03 chamber B uniformity measured 94.2% on recipe RX-7 with a 4.5s ramp, which is off spec.",
  "at": "2026-09-14T22:10:00Z",
  "deviceId": "PAD-07",
  "cleanroom": "CR-2",
  "tags": ["excursion", "follow-up"],
  "raw": "etch03 chmbr B unif 94.2% recipe RX-7 ramp 4.5s - off spec"
}

### Submit a finished shift report directly, bypassing FabPad
POST {{baseUrl}}/report
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "author": "K. Nagy",
  "shift": "Night 2026-09-14",
  "deviceId": "PAD-07",
  "cleanroom": "CR-2",
  "compiledAt": "2026-09-14T23:00:00Z",
  "noteCount": 4,
  "cloudUsed": true,
  "text": "Shift summary — four notes, all nominal or already actioned."
}

### The fleet-wide question without the browser — same call FabDesk's "Ask" button makes
POST {{baseUrl}}/query
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "question": "anything odd about ETCH-03 today?"
}
